WEBVTT

00:00:08.006 --> 00:00:10.951
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> When you’re having a drink together on a lazy Sunday afternoon,

00:00:10.951 --> 00:00:15.121
it seems like such a good idea. You’re the best of friends,

00:00:15.121 --> 00:00:18.799
you get on well, what could be better than going on holiday together?

00:00:19.227 --> 00:00:23.120
Well, before you get carried away and log on to Tripadvisor,

00:00:23.491 --> 00:00:25.677
let me give you one word of advice.

00:00:26.271 --> 00:00:26.987
Don’t.

00:00:27.897 --> 00:00:30.421
Holidays together are never a good idea.

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:33.567
Believe me, I know. I’ve been there.

00:00:34.081 --> 00:00:36.768
And I have a trail of broken friendships to prove it.

00:00:37.309 --> 00:00:40.735
Because, no matter how well you think you know your friends,

00:00:40.925 --> 00:00:46.088
you don’t really know them until you’ve lived under the same roof for a week.

00:00:46.191 --> 00:00:48.544
That’s roughly how long it takes to discover that

00:00:48.544 --> 00:00:52.571
your caring friend is really a hypochondriac control freak.

00:00:53.524 --> 00:00:56.688
One of the biggest problems with going on holiday with friends

00:00:56.688 --> 00:00:59.152
is the difference in expectations.

00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:04.561
You’re looking forward to relaxing on the beach with Joanna Trollope’s latest must-read novel,

00:01:04.736 --> 00:01:09.517
while their mission is to visit every ancient ruin within a 50-mile radius.

00:01:09.732 --> 00:01:12.785
Which is all fine if they don’t mind going it alone,

00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:18.066
but when they try to make you feel guilty for lying on your lounger, it’s a different story.

00:01:18.908 --> 00:01:19.897
A few years ago,

00:01:20.183 --> 00:01:24.256
we hired a motorhome on the west coast of Canada with another couple.

00:01:24.637 --> 00:01:28.053
Now, I’m not really a camping kind of girl,

00:01:28.053 --> 00:01:31.685
but since this motorhome was like the Waldorf on wheels,

00:01:31.685 --> 00:01:35.356
and North American campsites are known for all their mod cons,

00:01:35.681 --> 00:01:37.938
I thought that I’d be pretty safe.

00:01:38.573 --> 00:01:41.411
Unfortunately, what we didn’t realize is that

00:01:41.411 --> 00:01:46.556
our friends had decided this would be the ideal opportunity to get back to nature.

00:01:47.198 --> 00:01:52.556
They had booked the ultimate get-away-from-it-all campsite, high in the mountains.

00:01:53.001 --> 00:01:56.046
Not only were we getting away from civilization,

00:01:56.395 --> 00:02:00.597
we were also getting away from electricity, running water,

00:02:00.897 --> 00:02:03.472
and anything else that made life tolerable.

00:02:04.107 --> 00:02:08.027
Our friends sulked when I suggested that maybe we could compromise

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:11.964
and move on to a different campsite for the second half of the holiday.

00:02:12.551 --> 00:02:18.511
‘So, you want to give up all this fantastic scenery for a proper toilet?’ they sneered.

00:02:18.701 --> 00:02:22.350
Well, yes, that’s exactly what I wanted.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:23.987
Because, I have to tell you,

00:02:24.225 --> 00:02:29.409
no amount of spectacular scenery makes up for a smelly hole in the ground

00:02:29.461 --> 00:02:32.583
next to a sign saying ‘Beware of the Bears’.

00:02:33.408 --> 00:02:38.858
But if there’s one thing guaranteed to bring on a major holiday fall-out, it’s children.

00:02:39.532 --> 00:02:41.187
Your own can be bad enough,

00:02:41.291 --> 00:02:45.272
but a week with someone else’s hyperactive toddler is enough to turn

00:02:45.272 --> 00:02:48.900
even the most mild-mannered of us into Supernanny.

00:02:49.442 --> 00:02:52.756
And you try packing your own children off to bed at a reasonable hour

00:02:52.984 --> 00:02:55.763
when theirs are still bouncing off the ceiling at midnight,

00:02:55.788 --> 00:02:58.125
hyped up on fizzy drinks and video games!

00:02:59.038 --> 00:03:04.067
We knew our friends Amanda and Simon took a relaxed attitude towards parenting,

00:03:04.202 --> 00:03:06.230
but we didn’t realize just how relaxed

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:09.868
until we shared a villa in Spain with them and their ten-year-old son.

00:03:10.543 --> 00:03:15.190
‘Freddie has never heard the word “No” – it stifles his creativity,’

00:03:15.397 --> 00:03:20.935
they boasted to us proudly, as he hurled himself fully clothed into the swimming pool,

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:23.836
five minutes before we were due to go out to dinner.

00:03:24.508 --> 00:03:26.569
He certainly heard it that holiday,

00:03:26.688 --> 00:03:28.158
bellowed by my husband

00:03:28.253 --> 00:03:33.449
after Freddie tried to fire a catapult into our daughter’s face at point-blank range.

00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:35.767
He heard a few other choice words, too.

00:03:36.489 --> 00:03:40.153
Ten years on, I think Amanda and her husband still blame us for the fact that

00:03:40.153 --> 00:03:42.552
Freddie isn’t a concert pianist.

00:03:43.441 --> 00:03:47.281
It’s even worse when one of you has children and the other doesn’t.

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:52.270
I once spent a week in France, breastfeeding my newborn daughter in a wardrobe

00:03:52.507 --> 00:03:58.529
because our child-free friends hadn’t allowed for the vast amount of baggage a baby comes with

00:03:58.688 --> 00:04:01.454
and had allocated us the smallest room.

00:04:02.024 --> 00:04:04.896
And they were most put out that we couldn’t drop everything

00:04:04.897 --> 00:04:08.120
and hit the town at night, like in the old days.

00:04:08.715 --> 00:04:10.424
But how things change!

00:04:10.631 --> 00:04:14.444
A few years later, we went on holiday with the same friends,

00:04:14.555 --> 00:04:16.851
now proud parents themselves,

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:21.029
and we weren’t allowed to speak above a whisper for ten days.

00:04:21.877 --> 00:04:26.344
So, if it’s such a bad idea, why do I keep going back for more?

00:04:27.019 --> 00:04:30.565
It may be because holidays with friends are like childbirth:

00:04:30.978 --> 00:04:36.110
after a while, you forget the pain and it seems like a good idea to have another go.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:37.750
Which reminds me,

00:04:37.972 --> 00:04:42.402
my sister and her husband have just invited us to join them on a mini-break to Barcelona.

00:04:42.783 --> 00:04:47.732
Now, honestly, what could be nicer than a family get-together?